WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.340
WARREN BUFFETT:
Stocks vs bonds is one of the most hotly debated

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.049
topics within the investing world.

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:11.340
Some argue that any healthy diversified portfolio
should have bonds while others argue that

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.379
bonds have been completely useless in the
era of zero interest rates.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:21.050
One of the most ruthless critics of bonds
over the years has been none other than Warren

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.050
Buffett.

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:28.289
“If you had your choice between buying and
holding a 30-year bond for 30 years or holding

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:32.619
a basket of American stocks there's just no
question you're gonna do better owning stocks.”

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:37.770
“It’s very hard for an unproductive investment
to beat productive Investments over any long

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.890
period of time.”

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:44.149
“Long term bonds are a terrible investment.”

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:49.449
But despite these scathing remarks in the
past, it seems that Buffett has largely shifted

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.810
his tune over the past 12 months.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:57.240
In fact, he came into 2023 holding $95 billion
in treasury bills.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.510
And as of August, he was buying up $10 billion
worth of treasury bills every single week.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:08.700
For perspective, Buffett’s largest investment
is Apple where he holds $164 billion and his

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.090
second largest investment is Bank of America
where he holds $27 billion.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:18.860
So, Buffett’s bet on treasury bills square
places it as his second largest investment.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:23.310
In fact, he has even gone as far as selling
off his stock portfolio while continuing to

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.970
buy treasuries.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:30.549
This kind of makes Buffett seem like a hypocrite
but in his defense, his remarks against bonds

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:36.290
were made in a completely different investing
landscape in an era of 0% interest rates.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:40.790
Ever since the 2008 financial recession, the
Federal Reserve has kept interest rates at

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:45.079
near 0 levels in an effort to boost spending
and bolster the economy.

00:01:45.079 --> 00:01:50.700
But, last year, the fed made a 180 degree
pivot and started raising rates at a record

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.040
pace to historically high levels of over 5%.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.549
As such, treasury yields and corporate bond
yields have been consistently reaching new

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:01.020
highs over the past several months.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.259
Combine this with the worst inflation we’ve
seen in the past 40 years peaking at just

00:02:05.259 --> 00:02:10.550
over 9%, and it’s no wonder why investors
have adopted a vastly different outlook when

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.670
it comes to stocks vs bonds.

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:18.860
So, join me as we take a deep dive into the
case for stocks vs bonds in the era of high

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:22.390
inflation and high interest rates.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:30.080
CASE FOR STOCKS:
If this video piques your interest in bond

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.239
investing, please consider checking out our
upcoming app Silo in the description below.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:39.720
But anyway, starting off with the case for
stocks, likely the most obvious factor that

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:44.510
immediately meets the eye is the stock market
sell off over the past 2 years.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.910
Many household names sold off 50 to 75% or
even more.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:55.640
For example, Google sold off 45%, Meta sold
off 77%, Netflix sold off 78%, and even the

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.130
whole NASDAQ sold off 38%.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:02.890
Normally, this would be a disadvantage but
if you were consistently investing throughout

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:07.050
the downtrend, you would’ve been rewarded
with a massive recovery.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:14.470
For example, Netflix has rocketed 178%, Meta
has rocketed 272%, and some stocks have even

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:21.010
gone on to make new all time highs like Nvidia
who has grown 364% from the bottom.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:25.680
Now of course, no one knows when such rallies
will happen and trying to time the market

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.500
is a massive mistake.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:32.140
But, not investing in the market is also a
massive mistake.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:37.269
Usually, the best way to go about these situations
is to simply dollar cost average into your

00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:41.180
favorite companies and bet on their long term
growth.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:46.459
And in that vein, this strategy should not
change due rising or falling interest rates.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:51.739
With the S&amp;P 500 for example, just missing
the 10 best days over a 30 year period would

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:54.420
wipe out over half of your returns.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.980
So, if you already have a solid stock investing
plan in place, it’s best to stick with this

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:00.980
plan.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:06.090
Any other investments should be done in addition
to this plan, not in replacement of this plan.

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:11.000
Speaking of this massive recent recovery,
we have the second advantage for stocks which

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.489
is that stocks simply have potential for far
higher returns.

00:04:15.489 --> 00:04:19.459
Likely the highest returns that you can currently
earn with an investment grade corporate bond

00:04:19.459 --> 00:04:24.370
is 8% which fares quite well next to the S&amp;P
500.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:29.470
But when we take a look at individual stocks,
it’s a whole different story.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.880
If you were investing in Facebook, Apple,
Google, Netflix, and Nvidia over the past

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.800
10 years for example, you would’ve 5 to
10xed your money.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.680
Now of course, it could have just as easily
gone the other way.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.610
For example, other household names like Intel,
Cisco, and AT&amp;T, haven’t gone anywhere in

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:48.620
the past 23 years.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:55.160
But, that’s simply part of the stock game:
you take on higher risk for higher returns.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:59.780
If you’re ok with this risk, stocks are
still the way to go to potentially get outsized

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:02.110
returns of 5 or 10X.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:06.840
Speaking of outsized returns, these aren’t
the only reason to invest in stocks.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.530
If you’re a more conservative investor,
you could stick with dividend stocks which

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:12.919
sort of give you the best of both worlds.

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:16.040
There’s a few dozen companies that swear
by their dividends.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.210
Exxon for example hasn’t missed a dividend
payment since the 1800s.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.600
And it’s a similar case with Coca Cola,
Johnson &amp; Johnson, Proctor &amp; Gamble, and several

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:28.250
other companies as well.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:33.610
These companies are so consistent with their
dividends that it’s almost like a bond coupon

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:34.610
payment.

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:37.880
And the best part is that you’re not limited
to these dividends.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:42.850
If the company itself is growing consistently,
you may see strong stock returns as well as

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.900
was the case with Johnson &amp; Johnson.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:50.380
So, if you already have a solid stock investing
plan, are looking for higher returns, or are

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.860
interested in dividend stocks, there’s a
pretty strong case to be made to continue

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.190
investing in stocks.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:06:03.460
But, like any other investment, stocks have
their own set of disadvantages as well.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:12.090
GRAY AREA:
Starting off with the elephant in the room,

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:16.130
we currently have record-high inflation and
interest rates which have historically been

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:18.699
very bad for stock market returns.

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:21.990
Take 1968 to 1982 for example.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:26.560
During this time period, inflation peaked
over 10% on two separate occasions and interest

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.030
rates reached nearly 20%.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:32.729
Do you wanna guess what the S&amp;P 500 did during
this time period?

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:37.100
Well, if you guessed stay completely flat,
then you’d be exactly right.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.500
Throughout this period, the S&amp;P 500 spent
some time above $100 and a lot of time below

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:46.470
a $100 but for the most part, it couldn’t
really break past $100.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:51.180
To make things worse, this period also saw
4 major stock market crashes.

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:57.370
The first one was 37%, the second one was
50%, the third one was 20% and the fourth

00:06:57.370 --> 00:06:59.810
one was 28%.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:04.590
And if you thought things couldn’t get any
worse, well, we still haven’t accounted

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.169
for inflation.

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:13.050
Something that cost $1 in 1968 would cost
$2.77 by 1982, meaning that the value of your

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.560
dollar fell over 60%.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:22.430
In other words, by staying flat, the S&amp;P 500
also lost 60% of its real value and when you

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:27.260
take a look at how high inflation affects
companies, this really isn’t all that surprising.

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:32.120
Like all of us, companies also struggle during
periods of high inflation.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:37.130
All we see from the consumer side is prices
going up across the board but the thing to

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.889
keep in mind is that prices are also going
up on the backend for companies as well.

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:44.680
And this hurts them in two fronts.

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:49.419
For one, companies may not be able to raise
consumer prices as much as their backend prices

00:07:49.419 --> 00:07:52.440
are going up leaving them with lower profit
margins.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.270
And two, due to higher prices, less consumers
are willing to buy their products.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.270
But, all of that is only half the story.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.629
You see, when inflation starts getting out
of the control, the fed likes to step in and

00:08:04.629 --> 00:08:06.780
jack up interest rates to cool down the economy.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:12.310
And for high growth companies who rely on
debt and venture capital to keep growing,

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:14.379
this is a disaster.

00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:19.580
Given that much of tech is exactly this, high
inflation and high interest isn’t exactly

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.110
a great omen for the stock market.

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:27.210
Something else that isn’t a great omen is
the massive bull market that we just completed.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.800
It’s no secret that the stock market is
quite cyclical.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.290
It has boom periods and bust periods.

00:08:33.290 --> 00:08:40.089
The 70s was a bust period, the 80s and 90s
were a boom period, the 2000s was a bust period,

00:08:40.089 --> 00:08:42.510
and the 2010s was a boom period.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:48.850
In fact, between 2009 and 2021, the S&amp;P 500
grew over 600%.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:53.450
Combine this with a shifting investing landscape
and we could be heading into another bust

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:54.450
period.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:58.709
Now of course, past performance in not an
indicator of future results and you shouldn’t

00:08:58.709 --> 00:09:03.910
be trying to time the market but this may
be a good time to diversify your portfolio

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.990
into other investment classes if you haven’t
already.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:11.120
And that brings us into the case for bonds.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:20.190
CASE FOR BONDS:
Interestingly enough, the case for bonds is

00:09:20.190 --> 00:09:23.490
quite similar to the case AGAINST stocks.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:28.680
Just a couple weeks ago, the Federal Reserve
decided to pause their 18 month long rate-hike

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:29.680
bonanza.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.320
According the Fed, inflation statistics are
looking favorable meaning that there’s a

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.490
strong chance that we have a soft landing.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:40.360
This historic war on inflation has brought
the American economy to interest rates of

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.360
5.25-5.50%.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.570
Here are just a few examples that show the
effect of a higher federal funds rate.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:53.329
Mortgage rates are as high as 7.41%, Used
car loan rates are at 8.06%, New car loan

00:09:53.329 --> 00:09:59.700
rates are at 7.46%, Credit card interest rates
are at 20.71%, and so on.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:05.070
All of these examples have to do with us paying
higher rates but we can also use this situation

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:09.620
to our advantage and earn higher rates on
investments such as savings accounts, CDs,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.279
money markets, and of course bonds.

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:16.490
Just by keeping your money in an account,
you can earn 4.75% at Betterment, 4.50% at

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:23.880
SoFi, 4.30% at American Express, or 4.30%
at Capital One.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:27.080
You might be thinking, doesn’t the stock
market return 8-10%?

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:33.490
Well, surprisingly, for the first time in
a LONG time, S&amp;P 500 returns have matched

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.000
the yield of a 2-year treasury note.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:39.910
And this parity becomes even more significant
when we account for inflation.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:46.360
In 2022 for example, not only did inflation
peak over 9% but the S&amp;P 500 also sold off

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:47.950
as much as 27%.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:53.959
So, stock investors were feeling pain from
2 fronts: inflation and a sell off, and this

00:10:53.959 --> 00:10:57.209
is where bonds may be able to help.

00:10:57.209 --> 00:11:01.490
Bonds give you something the stock market
rarely can; stability.

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:05.430
Holding government or highly-rated corporate
bonds to maturity means consistent interest

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:10.050
payments, typically semi-annually, and a repayment
of your principal value.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:14.670
And just like how us individuals can get crucified
for missing those credit card payments or

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:19.250
mortgage payments, companies can also find
themselves in murky waters if they miss or

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:21.510
skip an interest payment on a bond.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:27.139
You see, while companies can raise money issuing
either stock or bonds, they have a contractual

00:11:27.139 --> 00:11:33.060
obligation to pay back bond holders and this
is captured in the pecking order.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:37.820
Companies are required to prioritize debt
known as senior and junior debt as their #1

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:38.820
priority.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:43.580
Conversely, dividends disbursements on common
stock is LAST priority.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:48.050
In other words, paying dividends is a good
faith effort while paying back bond holders

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.050
is a legal obligation.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:53.220
This is especially important if a company
ends up bankrupt.

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:58.149
In such a scenario, what happens is that the
assets the company does have gets liquidated

00:11:58.149 --> 00:12:02.690
into cash and distributed down the priority
ladder till there’s no cash left.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:08.160
Usually, the debt gets paid back in some form
of compensation while the stock becomes completely

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:09.160
worthless.

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:14.360
As such, the case for bonds often lies in
an environment where stability is more achievable

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.519
than growth.

00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:19.709
And the current tumultuous market with high
inflation and high interest is the perfect

00:12:19.709 --> 00:12:22.370
example of such an environment.

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:26.949
Investing in bonds means locking in rates
that get paid for the lifetime of the bond,

00:12:26.949 --> 00:12:30.960
whether that be 30 days, 2 years, or even
30 years.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.300
Conversely, savings account rates only last
as long as the fed keeps interest rates high

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.360
and stock market returns are not exactly predictable.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:43.320
So, if you’re looking for stable consistent
returns, it doesn’t get much better than

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.260
bonds.

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:52.800
THE BORING TRUTH:
Clicking on this video, a lot of you were

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:57.370
probably hoping to get a definitive answer
between bonds and stocks.

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:02.590
I’m sorry to disappoint but the truth is
that there is no definitive answer and Warren

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.019
Buffett has always known this.

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:10.149
While some of his statements and actions may
seem super against bonds or super pro bonds,

00:13:10.149 --> 00:13:15.321
the reality is that he’s always had a balance
stocks, bonds, and cash regardless of the

00:13:15.321 --> 00:13:16.910
investing environment.

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:22.050
For example, in the same clip that Buffett
claims that long term bonds are a terrible

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:26.709
investment at near 0 interest rates, he immediately
turns around and says that all of their money

00:13:26.709 --> 00:13:47.829
is in 

00:13:47.829 --> 00:13:52.199
treasury bills.

00:13:52.199 --> 00:14:01.930
So, the reality is that there is no one size
fits all and the answer of stocks vs bonds

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:06.589
is much more nuanced and dependent on your
age, income, net worth, risk tolerance, and

00:14:06.589 --> 00:14:07.880
slew of other factors.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:13.399
But, if you’re looking for a general rule,
the rule of 100 may be of value.

00:14:13.399 --> 00:14:17.579
The rule of 100 basically suggests that an
investors bond holdings should be directly

00:14:17.579 --> 00:14:19.240
proportional to their age.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:25.280
So, if you’re 30 years old for example,
you should have 30% in bonds and 70% in stocks.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:29.790
If you’re 40 years old, you should have
40% in bonds and 60% in stocks and so on and

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.019
so forth.

00:14:31.019 --> 00:14:35.250
This strategy ensures that when you’re younger
and have a higher risk appetite, most of your

00:14:35.250 --> 00:14:38.720
portfolio is invested in stocks with high
return potential.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.760
Conversely, when you’re older and value
stability and reliable income, it ensures

00:14:42.760 --> 00:14:46.160
that most of your portfolio is invested in
stable bonds.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:51.350
So, if you don’t own any bonds at all, it
may be a good idea to look into bonds whether

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:56.130
that’s through Silo or some other platform
especially with high inflation and high interest

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:57.130
rates.

00:14:57.130 --> 00:15:01.139
But, this isn’t to say that you should shy
away from any stock investing plan that you

00:15:01.139 --> 00:15:08.080
already have in place and that’s the truth
about stocks vs bonds in era of high interest.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.899
It looks like big tech companies can’t get
their hands on enough bonds.

00:15:11.899 --> 00:15:17.350
If you wanna learn more, check out this video.

